Using locally-sourced materials embeds products within regional material systems and cultural contexts while reducing transportation impacts and supporting local economic development. Products designed with local materials foster place-based identity and often connect to traditional knowledge about material properties and durability. This approach encourages designers to deeply understand material characteristics, seasonal availability, and regional supply chains. From a circular perspective, local material systems often have established reuse and recovery networks, making end-of-life recovery more efficient and culturally accepted.